-- Q3859: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls operated effectively throughout the full examination period","Whether user entities have implemented the required complementary controls","Whether the service organization''s financial statements are fairly presented","Whether controls are suitably designed to meet applicable control objectives"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320, tests of design evaluate whether controls, if operating as described, would be suitably designed to achieve the related control objectives or meet the applicable Trust Services Criteria. Design testing determines if the control is capable of addressing the identified risk.
Wrong (A): Testing whether controls operated effectively throughout the period describes tests of operating effectiveness, not tests of design.
Wrong (B): Evaluating complementary user entity controls is outside the scope of the service auditor''s tests of the service organization''s control design.
Wrong (C): Assessing fair presentation of financial statements is the objective of a financial statement audit, not a SOC engagement''s design testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3859;

-- Q3860: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of management, inspection of documentation, and observation","Reperformance of the control independently by the auditor","Statistical sampling of a representative transaction population","Reconciliation of account balances against supporting records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, testing control design typically involves inquiry of management, inspection of relevant documentation (policies, procedures, configurations), and observation of the control being performed. These procedures help the auditor understand how the control addresses the identified risk.
Wrong (B): Reperformance is used to test operating effectiveness by independently re-executing a control, not to evaluate design suitability.
Wrong (C): Statistical sampling tests populations of transactions for deviation rates and is an operating effectiveness procedure, not a design test.
Wrong (D): Reconciliation of account balances is a financial audit procedure unrelated to evaluating IT control design in a SOC engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3860;

-- Q3864: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Selecting every item in the population for complete testing coverage","Selecting a sample and testing each item for a specific characteristic like approval","Calculating the average dollar value of all transactions in the period","Testing only the largest transactions based on their monetary significance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 205 and AICPA Audit Sampling guidance, attribute sampling involves selecting a representative sample and examining each item for the presence or absence of a specific characteristic, such as documented approval. The results estimate the deviation rate in the entire population.
Wrong (A): Selecting every item describes census testing, not attribute sampling, which relies on a representative subset of the population.
Wrong (C): Calculating average dollar values describes variable sampling or monetary estimation, not the binary attribute testing approach.
Wrong (D): Testing only the largest transactions describes targeted or key-item testing, not the representative sampling that defines attribute sampling.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3864;

-- Q3865: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A higher expected deviation rate or a lower tolerable deviation rate","The control is automated and operates consistently without variation","The control operates only once per year during the period","The population of items being tested is very small"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls, larger sample sizes are required when the expected deviation rate is higher or the tolerable deviation rate is lower, particularly for critical controls where greater precision is needed. Prior exceptions increase the expected deviation rate.
Wrong (B): Automated controls with consistent execution typically require smaller samples due to reduced variability, not larger ones.
Wrong (C): Controls operating only once per year require testing every occurrence (one item), not a larger sample size.
Wrong (D): A very small population typically reduces sample size since fewer items exist to test, potentially requiring census testing instead.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3865;

-- Q3866: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To test operating effectiveness of the control over the full examination period","To gain understanding of the control by tracing a transaction end to end","To calculate the error rate across the entire transaction population","To evaluate the service organization''s overall financial performance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320, a walkthrough involves tracing a single transaction or event through the entire process from initiation to completion. The purpose is to confirm the auditor''s understanding, verify that described controls are in place, and identify where controls operate within the process.
Wrong (A): Testing operating effectiveness over the full period requires sampling multiple items, not tracing a single transaction through the process.
Wrong (C): Calculating error rates requires statistical sampling of multiple items, which is a different testing procedure from a walkthrough.
Wrong (D): Evaluating financial performance is outside the scope of a SOC engagement walkthrough, which focuses on understanding control processes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3866;

-- Q3867: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the exceptions since the majority of sampled items were properly approved","Automatically issue a qualified opinion because any exception requires qualification","Evaluate the nature, cause, and frequency to determine systemic failure or isolated deviation","Request management to retrospectively approve the three changes to cure the exceptions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls (Section 4: Evaluating Deviations), the auditor must evaluate each exception by assessing its nature, cause, frequency, and potential impact on the control objective before determining the effect on the opinion. A 12% deviation rate may indicate systemic weakness.
Wrong (A): Ignoring exceptions violates professional standards requiring the auditor to evaluate all identified deviations regardless of the overall pass rate.
Wrong (B): A qualified opinion is not automatic; the auditor must first investigate root causes and assess whether deviations represent systemic failure.
Wrong (D): Retrospective approval does not remediate the control failure; the change was deployed without approval, and after-the-fact documentation does not cure the deviation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3867;

-- Q3869: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sampling-based testing of multiple control occurrences","Benchmarking or baseline testing of the automated control configuration","Full population testing across the entire examination period","Risk-based testing focused on the highest-priority controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320, benchmarking is a testing approach for automated controls where the auditor verifies the control operates as configured at a point in time, then confirms through change management records that the configuration remained unchanged during the examination period.
Wrong (A): Sampling-based testing selects multiple items from a population, whereas benchmarking tests configuration at a single point in time.
Wrong (C): Full population testing examines every occurrence, which is unnecessary for automated controls with verified static configurations.
Wrong (D): Risk-based testing describes a planning methodology for prioritizing audit areas, not a specific technique for testing automated control configurations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3869;

-- Q3873: SOC Testing Controls [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control operated effectively because all four quarterly reviews were completed on schedule","The auditor should test only the March review since it has the strongest supporting evidence","The control was not designed properly and must be completely redesigned before next period","Three reviews lack follow-up evidence, indicating the control may not have operated effectively"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320.35 and AICPA TSC CC6.1, an effective access review must include documented evidence of follow-up and remediation of identified issues. The absence of follow-up documentation in three of four quarters indicates the control did not operate effectively throughout the period.
Wrong (A): Completing the reviews is necessary but insufficient; operating effectiveness requires documented follow-up on findings, which was missing in three quarters.
Wrong (B): The auditor must evaluate all occurrences of a quarterly control, not selectively test only the strongest instance.
Wrong (C): The control design (quarterly access reviews) is appropriate; the issue is operating effectiveness, not design inadequacy.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3873;

-- Q3876: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry, because the auditor is asking the tool to report on the system''s encryption state","Inspection, because the auditor examines actual configuration output against documented policy","Reperformance, because the auditor independently re-executes the encryption configuration process","Observation, because the auditor watches the scanning tool perform automated analysis in real time"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320.39, using a scanning tool to extract and examine system configuration settings against policy requirements constitutes inspection. The auditor is examining existing system output as evidence rather than re-executing a control or relying on management representations.
Wrong (A): Inquiry involves asking management about configurations; running a scanning tool produces direct objective evidence, not a verbal or written response.
Wrong (C): Reperformance requires the auditor to independently re-execute the control; the scan reads existing settings rather than reconfiguring encryption.
Wrong (D): Observation involves watching personnel perform a process in real time; running a technical tool to extract configuration data is inspection, not observation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3876;

-- Q3877: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Haphazard selection without a structured methodology","Systematic selection using a fixed interval with random start","Representative sampling to evaluate the attribute across the population","Census testing of every item in the full annual population"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AT-C Section 320.40 and the AICPA Audit Sampling Guide, selecting items from a population and testing them for specific attributes such as documented approval constitutes representative sampling. The observed deviation rate is projected to draw conclusions about the entire population.
Wrong (A): Haphazard selection describes only the method of choosing items, not the overall attribute testing approach being employed.
Wrong (B): Systematic selection is a specific selection technique, not a description of the complete testing procedure that includes evaluating attributes.
Wrong (D): Census testing examines every item in the population; selecting 25 from 1,200 is clearly a sampling approach, not a complete examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3877;

-- Q3879: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["25 or more items, based on the auditor''s risk assessment","1 item, since only a single day needs verification","5 items selected at random from the examination period","All 365 occurrences must be tested without exception"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls (Appendix B), professional guidance recommends a minimum sample of 25 items for daily controls over a 12-month Type II period, adjusted based on risk assessment and expected deviation rate. This provides high statistical confidence.
Wrong (B): Testing only 1 item from 365 daily occurrences provides no statistical basis for concluding on operating effectiveness over the full period.
Wrong (C): A sample of 5 items is insufficient to draw reliable conclusions about a population of 365 daily control occurrences.
Wrong (D): Testing all 365 occurrences is census testing, which is unnecessarily resource-intensive when statistical sampling at 25 items provides sufficient assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3879;

-- Q3881: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of knowledgeable personnel about the change process","Inspection and corroboration of evidence between two independent sources","Observation of the change management process being performed","Walkthrough tracing a single change request from start to finish"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 205.29, inspection involves examining records and documents to obtain audit evidence. Comparing a production change log against the change management system to verify approvals constitutes inspection with dual-source corroboration, which is more reliable than a single source.
Wrong (A): Inquiry involves asking personnel questions; this procedure examines documentary evidence from two systems rather than gathering verbal representations.
Wrong (C): Observation involves watching a process being performed in real time, not reviewing recorded evidence from logs and systems after the fact.
Wrong (D): A walkthrough traces a single transaction end to end to gain understanding; this procedure systematically compares records across two independent sources.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3881;

-- Q3882: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A sample of 25 occurrences from the examination period","No testing is required for controls that operate annually","Every occurrence within the period, which for annual controls is one","A minimum of 10 occurrences regardless of the control frequency"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.34), for controls that operate infrequently such as annually, the service auditor must test every occurrence within the examination period. For an annual control, that single instance must be tested in its entirety.
Wrong (A): A sample of 25 applies to frequently occurring controls like daily or weekly; annual controls have too few occurrences for statistical sampling.
Wrong (B): All controls within scope must be tested regardless of frequency; annual controls are not exempt from testing requirements.
Wrong (D): There is no blanket minimum of 10 occurrences; the standard requires testing every occurrence for infrequently operating controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3882;

-- Q3887: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Auditor''s report, management''s assertion, system description, and tests with results","Only the independent service auditor''s report and a summary conclusion","The service organization''s audited financial statements and footnotes","The user entity''s internal audit findings and management letter"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), a SOC 1 Type II report contains four main sections: the independent service auditor''s report (opinion), management''s assertion, the system description, and the description of tests of controls and results. Together these provide comprehensive information for user entities.
Wrong (B): A SOC 1 report includes more than just the auditor''s report and a conclusion; it requires management''s assertion, system description, and detailed test results.
Wrong (C): A SOC 1 report addresses controls relevant to user entities'' financial reporting, not the service organization''s own audited financial statements.
Wrong (D): User entity internal audit findings are produced by the user entity, not included in the service organization''s SOC 1 report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3887;

-- Q3888: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Fair presentation of system description, control design suitability, and operating effectiveness","The service auditor''s detailed testing methodology and planned sample sizes","The user entity''s satisfaction with the service organization''s performance","The competitive market position of the service organization in its industry"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, management''s assertion is a written statement in which the service organization''s management asserts that the system description fairly presents the system, controls are suitably designed, and for Type II, controls operated effectively throughout the specified period.
Wrong (B): Testing methodology and sample sizes are determined by the service auditor, not addressed in management''s assertion about the system and controls.
Wrong (C): User entity satisfaction is outside the scope of a SOC engagement; management''s assertion addresses system description accuracy and control effectiveness.
Wrong (D): Competitive market position is a business strategy matter unrelated to the SOC report''s focus on controls and system description.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3888;

-- Q3889: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The report includes a complete opinion on the subservice organization''s controls","The report identifies the carved-out functions and excludes them from the opinion","The report excludes any mention of the subservice organization entirely","The report automatically includes a qualified opinion due to the carve-out"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.A27), the carve-out method excludes the subservice organization''s controls from scope. The report identifies the subservice organization and describes its functions, but the opinion does not extend to its controls. User entities must evaluate those controls independently.
Wrong (A): The carve-out method specifically excludes subservice controls from scope; a complete opinion on those controls would require the inclusive method.
Wrong (C): The report must identify and describe the subservice organization and its functions even though the controls are excluded from the opinion scope.
Wrong (D): A carve-out does not automatically result in a qualified opinion; it is a standard scoping approach, and the opinion addresses only the in-scope controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3889;
